Give the engine a state directory of its own, without moving the mounts.

anylinuxfs keeps everything it needs under the invoking user's home: the Linux
image and its configuration in ~/.anylinuxfs, its logs in ~/Library/Logs. That
is right for a program somebody installed themselves -- there is one of it, and
the directory is its own.

An application that carries the engine inside it is a different case. Several
can be installed at once -- a release, a beta, and anylinuxfs itself, installed
by whoever was using it first -- and each ships the image version its own engine
was built against. One directory between them means each finds an image it did
not put there, and either runs against one it was not built for or replaces the
others'. Every one of those is somebody's drive failing to open for a reason
nothing on screen could explain.

ANYLINUXFS_HOME moves that state and only that state. The home directory itself
is still resolved from the invoking user, because it decides something else
entirely: where an unprivileged mount appears. Drives belong in ~/Volumes,
where the person can find them, and not wherever an application keeps its
files.

So this separates the two meanings the home directory had:

  home_dir    the person's home. Mount points, and nothing else.
  state_dir   where the image, the configuration and the logs live.

Unset, state_dir is home_dir and nothing changes: a copy installed on its own
behaves exactly as it always has. Set, an application can give the engine a
directory nothing else writes to, and drives still mount where they always did.

The image is unpacked by a second program, written in Go, and the first version
of this patch changed only the Rust. So the profile, the configuration and the
logs moved and the image did not: with the variable set to an application's own
directory, `init` still wrote ~/.anylinuxfs/alpine, holding oci, rootfs, an
mtree and umoci.json. The application then looked in its own directory, found
nothing, unpacked again, and told somebody their drive would not open -- for as
long as the app kept running, since only a fresh launch moved what was left.

Both programs read the variable now.

--- a/anylinuxfs/src/main.rs
+++ b/anylinuxfs/src/main.rs
@@ -176,6 +176,21 @@
     let home_dir = utils::home_dir_from_uid(invoker_uid)
